  <main id="main">
    <section class="phero" aria-labelledby="th-title">
      <div class="wrap phero__in">
        <div>
          <ol class="crumbs"><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/#services">Services</a></li><li>Thalassemia Treatment</li></ol>
          <h1 id="th-title" data-fx="wipe">Thalassemia Treatment in <span class="grad-text">Bangalore</span></h1>
          <p class="phero__sub" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.15s">Thalassemia is an inherited blood disorder in which the body cannot make enough healthy hemoglobin. Low levels cause anemia, tiredness and, in severe forms, a lifelong need for blood transfusions. The good news: thalassemia can be managed well, and for many children it can be cured.</p>
          <div class="hero__acts" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.25s">
            <a class="btn btn--grad" href="/contact?topic=thalassemia">Book a consultation<svg class="ic ic--go"><use href="#i-go"/></svg></a>
            <a class="btn btn--line" href="tel:+917899756677"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-phone"/></svg>+91 78997 56677</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="phero__pic phero__pic--art" data-fx="iris" style="--d:.15s">
          <img src="/assets/images/illustrations/thalassemia-cells.svg" alt="Illustration of small, pale red blood cells and a DNA strand, representing inherited thalassemia" width="400" height="400" fetchpriority="high">
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="th-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="th-what" data-fx="wipe">What is <span class="grad-text">thalassemia?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Hemoglobin is made of two kinds of protein chains, called alpha and beta. In thalassemia, a change in the genes means the body makes too little of one chain. Red blood cells then become small, weak and break down early, which leads to anemia.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Thalassemia is not an infection and not a cancer. It is passed from parents to children through genes, and it is present from birth.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">India has more children with thalassemia major than any other country: about 1 to 1.5 lakh children live with it, and 10,000 to 15,000 more are born every year (Union Health Ministry). With the right thalassemia treatment in Bangalore, children and adults with thalassemia can grow, study, work and live full lives.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>How is thalassemia inherited?</h3><p class="box__sub">Thalassemia passes down when both parents carry a faulty gene. Carriers are usually healthy and often don’t know they carry it.</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>If both parents are carriers:</b> each pregnancy has a 1 in 4 chance of a child with thalassemia major, a 2 in 4 chance of a carrier child and a 1 in 4 chance of an unaffected child.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>If only one parent is a carrier:</b> children cannot get thalassemia major, but each child has a 1 in 2 chance of being a carrier.</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="th-types">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Types</p><h2 class="title" id="th-types" data-fx="wipe">Types of <span class="grad-text">thalassemia</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it means</th><th>Treatment needed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Thalassemia minor (trait or carrier)</b></td><td data-label="What it means">One faulty gene. Usually no symptoms or only mild anemia.</td><td data-label="Treatment needed">Usually none. Important for marriage and pregnancy planning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Thalassemia intermedia</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Moderate anemia that starts later in childhood.</td><td data-label="Treatment needed">Regular check-ups; transfusions only at times.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Thalassemia major (Cooley’s anemia)</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Two faulty beta genes. Severe anemia from early childhood.</td><td data-label="Treatment needed">Regular transfusions and iron removal for life, or a bone marrow transplant.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>Alpha thalassemia</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Ranges from silent carrier to HbH disease to a severe form before birth.</td><td data-label="Treatment needed">Depends on the type; many need no treatment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type"><b>HbE beta thalassemia</b></td><td data-label="What it means">A mix of HbE and beta thalassemia, more common in eastern India.</td><td data-label="Treatment needed">Ranges from mild to transfusion-dependent.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="th-sym">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Signs &amp; symptoms</p><h2 class="title" id="th-sym" data-fx="wipe">Signs and symptoms of <span class="grad-text">thalassemia</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Thalassemia minor usually causes no symptoms. Children with thalassemia major usually show signs between 6 months and 2 years of age:</p>
        </div><ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg><b>Pale skin &amp; tiredness</b><span>Looking pale and tiring easily</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg><b>Poor feeding</b><span>Irritability and slow weight gain</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><b>Jaundice</b><span>Yellow eyes or skin and dark urine</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Swollen tummy</b><span>From an enlarged spleen or liver</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><b>Slow growth</b><span>And delayed puberty</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-bone"/></svg><b>Bone changes</b><span>In the face and skull, if treatment starts late</span></li></ul><div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-adult"/></svg>Thalassemia symptoms in adults</h3><p class="box__sub">Adults with thalassemia minor may have mild, lifelong anemia that does not improve with iron tablets. Adults with thalassemia intermedia may have tiredness, bone pain, gallstones or an enlarged spleen.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg>When should you see a thalassemia specialist?</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Your baby or child is pale, feeding poorly or not gaining weight</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Your blood report shows small red cells or low hemoglobin that does not improve with iron</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>You or your partner is a thalassemia carrier and you are planning marriage or a pregnancy</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>A family member has thalassemia</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Your child needs blood transfusions and you want to know about a cure</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="th-dx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="th-dx" data-fx="wipe">How is thalassemia <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Dr. Neema Bhat may advise some of these thalassemia diagnostic tests:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Complete Blood Count (CBC)</b><p>Shows low hemoglobin and small red cells (low MCV and MCH).</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Peripheral blood smear</b><p>Shows the shape of the red cells under a microscope.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>HPLC or hemoglobin electrophoresis</b><p>The main thalassemia test. It measures HbA2 and HbF and confirms carriers and thalassemia major.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Iron studies (ferritin)</b><p>To tell thalassemia minor apart from iron deficiency, which looks similar on a CBC.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>DNA (genetic) test</b><p>Finds the exact gene change. Useful for family planning and prenatal testing.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>HLA typing</b><p>Checks whether a brother or sister is a matching donor for a bone marrow transplant.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Prenatal testing</b><p>If both parents are carriers, a test at 10 to 12 weeks of pregnancy (CVS) can show whether the baby is affected.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="th-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="th-tx" data-fx="wipe">Thalassemia treatment <span class="grad-text">options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your thalassemia treatment in Bangalore depends on the type and how severe it is. Thalassemia minor usually needs no treatment. For thalassemia major, care has four parts:</p>
        </div><div class="tsteps"><div class="tstep" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><h3>Regular blood transfusions</h3><p class="box__sub">Most children with thalassemia major need safe, filtered blood every 2 to 4 weeks for life. Regular transfusions keep hemoglobin at a healthy level, so the child grows normally and the bones do not change shape.</p></div></div><div class="tstep" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><h3>Iron chelation therapy</h3><p class="box__sub">Every transfusion adds iron that the body cannot remove. Over time this iron builds up in the heart, liver and glands. Iron chelation medicines (deferasirox and deferiprone tablets, or deferoxamine injections) remove the extra iron and protect these organs. They must be taken every day.</p></div></div><div class="tstep" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><h3>Regular monitoring</h3><p class="box__sub">Serum ferritin is checked every few months. Older children and adults also need yearly heart and liver MRI scans (T2*) to measure iron, along with tests for growth, thyroid, diabetes and bone health.</p></div></div><div class="tstep" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><h3>Supportive care</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Folic acid, calcium and vitamin D as advised</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Vaccines, including hepatitis B</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Hydroxyurea for some people with thalassemia intermedia or HbE beta thalassemia</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Newer medicines that can reduce transfusion needs in some adults</span></li></ul></div></div></div><p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Do not take iron tablets unless your doctor confirms you are low in iron.</b> People with thalassemia, including carriers, can build up too much iron.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="th-bmt">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">A cure</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="th-bmt" data-fx="wipe">Bone marrow transplant: <span class="grad-text">the cure for thalassemia</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">A bone marrow (stem cell) transplant is currently the only established cure for thalassemia major in India. Healthy stem cells from a matched donor replace the patient’s bone marrow, so the body starts making normal red blood cells. After a successful transplant, the child no longer needs transfusions or iron chelation.</p>
            <div class="hero__acts" data-fx="fade"><a class="btn btn--line" href="/bone-marrow-transplant">About bone marrow transplant<svg class="ic ic--go"><use href="#i-go"/></svg></a></div>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg>What families should know</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>The best donor is a fully matched brother or sister.</b> Each sibling has a 1 in 4 chance of being a full match, so HLA typing of the whole family is the first step.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Earlier is better.</b> Results are best when the child is young, has had good chelation and has little iron damage to the liver.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Other donors:</b> if no sibling matches, a matched unrelated donor from a donor registry, or a half-matched parent, may be possible in an experienced centre.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Gene therapy</b> is approved in some countries and is being studied in India, but it is not yet routinely available here.</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="th-cost">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="th-cost"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg>Cost of thalassemia treatment in Bangalore</h3><p class="box__sub">Costs depend on the type of thalassemia and the treatment chosen:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Lifelong transfusion care:</b> transfusions every few weeks, daily chelation medicines and regular tests. This is an ongoing monthly cost for life.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Bone marrow transplant:</b> a one-time cost that usually ranges from ₹15 to ₹25 lakh in India, depending on the donor type and the child’s condition.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Carrier test (HPLC):</b> a simple, low-cost blood test.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">The clinic can give you a personalised estimate after the first consultation.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Financial help and free treatment options</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Thalassemia Bal Sewa Yojana:</b> Coal India’s scheme gives up to ₹10 lakh towards a bone marrow transplant for eligible children aged 12 or younger, from families earning up to ₹8 lakh a year, at empanelled hospitals.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Day care and NGO support:</b> Bangalore has thalassemia day care centres and patient groups that help with transfusions and medicines, such as the Thalassaemia and Sickle Cell Society of Bangalore.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Insurance and CSR funds:</b> some insurance policies and company CSR programmes support transplant costs. The team can guide you through the paperwork.</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="th-comp">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Complications</p><h2 class="title" id="th-comp" data-fx="wipe">Complications of <span class="grad-text">thalassemia</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Without regular treatment, or if iron is not removed well, thalassemia can cause:</p>
        </div><ul class="conds"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Heart problems</b><p>An irregular heartbeat and heart failure, from iron overload.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>Liver damage</b><p>And gallstones.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg>Hormone problems</b><p>Diabetes, thyroid problems and delayed puberty.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg>Weak bones</b><p>Osteoporosis and bone changes in the face.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Enlarged spleen</b><p>And a higher risk of infections.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Slow growth</b><p>In children.</p></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Good transfusion care, daily chelation and regular check-ups prevent most of these problems.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="th-prev">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="th-prev"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Preventing thalassemia: carrier test before marriage and pregnancy</h3><p class="box__sub">Thalassemia major can be prevented. A simple HPLC blood test shows whether you are a carrier. It is best done:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Before marriage, especially if someone in the family has thalassemia</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Before planning a pregnancy, or early in pregnancy</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>For brothers and sisters of anyone with thalassemia</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">If both partners are carriers, genetic counselling and a prenatal test at 10 to 12 weeks of pregnancy can show whether the baby is affected, so families can plan with full information.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Living well with thalassemia</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Keep to the transfusion schedule and never skip chelation medicines</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Eat a balanced diet; avoid iron-rich supplements and tonics unless prescribed</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Stay active; most children can go to school and play sports</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Track ferritin, growth and heart checks in one file and bring it to every visit</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Connect with other families through local thalassemia support groups</span></li></ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

