        </div>
        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">Can thalassemia be cured permanently?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. A bone marrow transplant can cure thalassemia major permanently, especially when it is done early with a matched sibling donor. Without a transplant, thalassemia is managed lifelong with transfusions and iron chelation.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">What is the best treatment for thalassemia major?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">For children with a matched donor, a bone marrow transplant is the best treatment because it is curative. For others, regular transfusions with good iron chelation give a long and active life.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">How often do thalassemia patients need blood?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Most people with thalassemia major need a transfusion every 2 to 4 weeks. The exact gap depends on their hemoglobin level, age and weight.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">Is thalassemia minor dangerous?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Thalassemia minor usually causes no symptoms or only mild anemia and needs no treatment. It matters mainly for family planning, because two carriers can have a child with thalassemia major.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">Can a thalassemia minor person get married?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. A carrier can marry anyone. If both partners are carriers, they should have genetic counselling and prenatal testing in each pregnancy.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">How long do thalassemia major patients live?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">With regular transfusions, daily chelation and good follow-up, many people with thalassemia major now live well into adulthood, study, work and have families. A successful transplant offers a normal life expectancy.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">What kind of doctor treats thalassemia?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">A hematologist, especially a pediatric hematologist with bone marrow transplant experience, treats thalassemia. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US-trained hematologist in Bangalore who manages both transfusion care and transplant.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">What is the thalassemia treatment cost in India?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Lifelong transfusion care is an ongoing monthly cost. A bone marrow transplant usually costs ₹15 to ₹25 lakh, and schemes such as Thalassemia Bal Sewa Yojana can cover up to ₹10 lakh for eligible children.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">Can I get free or low-cost treatment for thalassemia in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. Government schemes, CSR funds and NGO-run day care centres in Bangalore help with transfusions, medicines and transplant costs. The team can help you check which ones you qualify for.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">Is thalassemia a blood cancer?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Thalassemia is an inherited problem with hemoglobin production. It is not cancer and it is not contagious.</p></details></div>
      </div>
    </section>

